.SH NAME
ktm-guide-enums \- Enums (chapter 14 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
An enum is a set of named integer constants of its own type. Its members are always written with the enum's name in front, \fBColor.red\fR, so two enums can have members of the same name. This chapter covers declaring enums, their values and underlying type, using members as constants, flags, conversions to and from integers, switching on an enum, names at run time, and enums as error codes.
.SH Declaring an enum
.PP
\fBenum Name { members };\fR declares the type \fBName\fR and its members. A member is used as \fBName.member\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

i32 main() {
    Color c = Color.green;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", Color.red, c, Color.blue);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 1 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
The bare member name isn't in scope outside the enum:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", green);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
`green` has not been defined
.EE
.RE
.PP
So members of different enums never collide:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Light { off, on };
enum Fan { off, low, high };

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d\en", Light.on, Fan.high);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Enums are declared at the top level of a file.
.IP \(bu 2
A variable of an enum type, like any variable, starts at zero, which is the first member when it has no value of its own.
.SH Values
.PP
The first member is 0 and each next one is the previous plus 1, unless it is given a value:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Status { ok = 200, created, accepted, notFound = 404, gone };

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d %d\en", Status.ok, Status.created, Status.accepted);
    printf("%d %d\en", Status.notFound, Status.gone);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
200 201 202
404 405
.EE
.RE
.PP
Values can be negative, and a member's value can use earlier members without the prefix:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Temp { freezing = \-10, cold = 0, warm = 20, hot };
enum Size { small = 1, medium = small * 4, large = medium * 4 };

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", Temp.freezing, Temp.cold, Temp.warm, Temp.hot);
    printf("%d %d %d\en", Size.small, Size.medium, Size.large);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
\-10 0 20 21
1 4 16
.EE
.RE
.SS The underlying type
.PP
An enum is stored as an \fBi32\fR unless \fB: Type\fR after its name picks another integer type:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };
enum Status : u16 { ok = 200, notFound = 404 };
enum Big : i64 { huge = 5000000000 };

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d %d\en", sizeof(Color), sizeof(Status), sizeof(Big));
    printf("%d\en", Big.huge);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4 2 8
5000000000
.EE
.RE
.PP
A member that doesn't fit the type is an error:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Tiny : u8 { a, b = 255, c };

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
value 256 does not fit in `u8`
.EE
.RE
.SH Members are constants
.PP
\fBName.member\fR is a constant expression, so it can size an array, initialise a \fBconst\fR or a global, and label a \fBcase\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue, count };

u8 *names[Color.count] = {"red", "green", "blue"};
const i64 LAST = Color.blue;

i32 main() {
    printf("%d names, last %d\en", sizeof(names) / sizeof(u8 *), LAST);
    printf("%s\en", names[Color.green]);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3 names, last 2
green
.EE
.RE
.PP
A last member like \fBcount\fR above is a common way to know how many members there are, as long as the others have the default values 0, 1, 2 ...
.SH Switching on an enum
.PP
A \fBswitch\fR with one \fBcase\fR per member reads naturally; a case doesn't fall through into the next (see switch (see ktm-guide-switch(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

u8 *mood(Color c) {
    switch (c) {
        case Color.red:   return "warm";
        case Color.green: return "calm";
        case Color.blue:  return "cool";
    }
    return "?";
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%s %s %s\en", mood(Color.red), mood(Color.green), mood(Color.blue));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
warm calm cool
.EE
.RE
.SH Conversions
.PP
An enum value converts to an integer by itself, in assignments, arithmetic and comparisons:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

i32 main() {
    Color c = Color.blue;
    i64 n = c;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", n, c * 10, c > Color.red);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 20 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
The other way needs a cast: an integer isn't an enum value until it is said to be one.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

i32 main() {
    Color c = 1;
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Cannot convert `i64` to enum `Color` without a cast
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

Color next(Color c) {
    return (Color)((c + 1) % 3);
}

i32 main() {
    Color c = (Color)2;
    printf("%d %d\en", c, next(c));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 0
.EE
.RE
.PP
The cast doesn't check the number: \fB(Color)7\fR is a \fBColor\fR with no member of that value.
.PP
Arithmetic assignment (\fB+=\fR, \fB++\fR) on an enum variable is refused for the same reason; cast, compute, and cast back:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

i32 main() {
    Color c = Color.red;
    c++;
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Cannot do arithmetic assignment on enum `Color`
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

i32 main() {
    for (Color c = Color.red; c <= Color.blue; c = (Color)(c + 1)) {
        printf("%d ", c);
    }
    printf("\en");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 1 2 
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two different enums don't mix: one doesn't convert to the other, and comparing them is an error.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green };
enum Size { small, large };

i32 main() {
    Color c = Size.large;
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Cannot convert `Size` to enum `Color` without a cast
.EE
.RE
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green };
enum Size { small, large };

i32 main() {
    return Color.green == Size.large;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
Cannot compare enum `Color` with enum `Size`
.EE
.RE
.SH Flags
.PP
Members whose values are powers of two can be combined with \fB|\fR and tested with \fB&\fR\&. The combination is an integer, not a member:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Perm { read = 1, write = 2, exec = 4, all = read | write | exec };

i32 main() {
    i64 p = Perm.read | Perm.write;
    printf("can write: %d\en", (p & Perm.write) != 0);
    printf("can exec: %d\en", (p & Perm.exec) != 0);
    printf("all: %d\en", Perm.all);
    p &= ~Perm.write;
    printf("after removing write: %d\en", p);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
can write: 1
can exec: 0
all: 7
after removing write: 1
.EE
.RE
.SH Enums in structs
.PP
An enum field takes the enum's size; a struct initialiser list takes a member for it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Suit { hearts, spades };

struct Card {
    Suit suit;
    i64 rank;
};

i32 main() {
    Card c = {Suit.spades, 12};
    printf("%d %d, %d bytes\en", c.suit, c.rank, sizeof(Card));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 12, 16 bytes
.EE
.RE
.PP
An enum can't have methods of its own; a function that takes it does the same job:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green };

void Color.print() {
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
only a struct or union can have methods
.EE
.RE
.SH Names at run time
.PP
\fBprintf\fR prints an enum's number. \fBreflect.name\fR gives the member's name, and \fBreflect.enumValue\fR finds the value of a name (see Reflection (see ktm-guide-reflection(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import reflect;

enum Role { guest, member, admin = 10 };

i32 main() {
    Role r = Role.admin;
    printf("%d %s\en", r, reflect.name(r));
    i64 v;
    if (reflect.enumValue(reflect.typeOf(Role), "member", &v)) {
        printf("member is %d\en", v);
    }
    printf("[%s]\en", reflect.name((Role)7));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
10 admin
member is 1
[]
.EE
.RE
.PP
A value that isn't a member has the empty name. JSON and YAML write an enum field as its member's name and read it back the same way (see Attributes (see ktm-guide-attributes(7))).
.SH Enums as error codes
.PP
An enum is the usual code of an error (see Errors (see ktm-guide-errors(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum ParseError { empty, notNumber };

i64 parse(u8[] s) {
    if (!s.len) {
        throw(ParseError.empty, "empty input");
    }
    i64 n = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < s.len; i++) {
        u8 c = s[i];
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
            throw(ParseError.notNumber, "bad digit '%c'", c);
        }
        n = n * 10 + c \- '0';
    }
    return n;
}

i32 main() {
    u8[] inputs[3] = {"42", "", "4x"};
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        try {
            printf("%d\en", parse(inputs[i]));
        } catch (Error e) {
            printf("error %d: %s\en", e.code, e.message);
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42
error 0: empty input
error 1: bad digit 'x'
.EE
.RE
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenum Name { a, b = 5, c };\fR declares a type and its members, at the top level of a file.
.IP \(bu 2
Members count up from 0, or from the last given value; a value may use earlier members.
.IP \(bu 2
The underlying type is \fBi32\fR; \fBenum Name : u16 { ... }\fR picks another integer type, and a member that doesn't fit it is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBName.member\fR is a constant expression; the bare member name is not in scope outside.
.IP \(bu 2
An enum converts to an integer by itself; an integer or another enum needs a cast to become one, and a cast doesn't check the value.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB++\fR, \fB+=\fR and the like don't apply to enum variables; two enums can't be compared.
.IP \(bu 2
An enum has no methods. \fBreflect.name\fR and \fBreflect.enumValue\fR map members and names.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ktm-guide (7),
.BR ktm-guide-methods (7),
.BR ktm-guide-errors (7)
